<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Varangians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Varangians were Scandinavians, mostly from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, who travelled the rivers of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the 9th to the 11th centuries as traders, raiders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. They opened the route from the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a> founded the ruling dynasty of the Rus'. From the late 10th century Varangians also served the Byzantine emperors as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varangians.jpg" alt="Varangians" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Briangotts · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varangian_routes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Varangian comes through Greek and Old East Slavic from Old Norse væringi, probably meaning a sworn companion, extended to a foreigner who has taken service with a new lord by a treaty of fealty. Geography leads many to argue that most Varangians who settled in the lands of the eastern Baltic and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> came from the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. It has also been argued that the word does not appear in primary sources until the eleventh century, and that the name Rus' had earlier been used broadly for Scandinavians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> first mentions Varangians as exacting tribute from Slavic and Finnic tribes in 859. In the 9th century the Rus' operated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, selling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, honey and slaves and receiving Arabic silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>; hoards of Baghdad-minted coins have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, particularly on Gotland. Later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> routes overtook it in importance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</a> described the Rus' he met on the Volga in 921 and 922. Raids included an expedition of 913 on 500 ships that pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a>, and a capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda</a> in 943.</p><p>Raiding and trade also led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The Rus' attacked it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 860, though the result is disputed, and rulers of Kiev launched a naval expedition in 907, an abortive campaign in 941 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a>, an invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in 968 to 971. Militarily the Varangians were usually beaten by Byzantine forces, partly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a>. From at least the early 10th century many served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in the Byzantine army, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> served as bodyguard of the emperors from the 10th to the 14th century. It kept an almost entirely Norse character until the late 11th century, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> it increasingly took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>. By the late 13th century its members were largely assimilated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen">Västgötalagen</a> declared that no one could inherit while staying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a measure meant to curb enlistment.</p><p>Most Varangians converted from Norse paganism to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a>, culminating in the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> in 988. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> declined, the flow of Scandinavians to Rus' stopped and they gradually assimilated into Slavic culture by the late 11th century. Runestones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, such as the Greece Runestones, commemorate those who died abroad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Varangians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1038 a Byzantine army under George Maniaces, with a corps of Normans and <b>Varangians</b> including Harald Hardrada, crossed the strait of Messina and took Syracuse, but a Muslim counter-offensive won back all the cities lost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Scandinavian traders and warriors, known as Rus' or <b>Varangians</b>, moved down the Volkhov, Dnieper and Volga to exchange furs and slaves for Byzantine goods and Islamic silver, and a Rus' polity, sometimes called the Rus' Khaganate, existed in the north by the 830s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Viking Age, Swedish traders and warriors, often called <b>Varangians</b> or Rus, travelled the rivers of Russia to Byzantium and the Caliphate, and Birka on Lake Mälaren was a major trading town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval writers often used the word Goths for any Germanic people of eastern Europe, such as the <b>Varangians</b>, and some even for Slavic speakers.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east, Scandinavians known as Rus and <b>Varangians</b> travelled the rivers of Russia and Ukraine to Constantinople, where Varangians served in the emperor’s guard; a Viking named Halfdan carved his name in runes on a balustrade of the gallery of Hagia Sophia.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>English and Frankish chroniclers called them Northmen, Danes or heathens, the Irish called them Gaill, foreigners, and in the east they were Rus’ and <b>Varangians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
